---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';

const faq = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does AAAA stand for in DNS?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "In DNS, the AAAA record full form stands for a fourfold Address record (pronounced \"Quad-A\"). The IETF named the 128-bit IPv6 address record AAAA (32 bits x 4 = 128 bits) because a single A record stores a 32-bit IPv4 address."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I need both an A record and an AAAA record for my website?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. Publish both an A record (for IPv4) and an AAAA record (for IPv6) on the same hostname when your web server or CDN supports IPv6. This dual-stack setup lets visitors on modern mobile and fiber IPv6 networks connect directly while keeping full compatibility for IPv4-only users."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if my domain only has an A record and no AAAA record?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "In practice, your website still works normally for 99.9% of internet users. Almost all consumer ISPs and mobile carriers run dual-stack networks or NAT64 gateways, so when a visitor's browser sees no AAAA record, it immediately connects to your IPv4 A record with zero error."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why does my website load for some people and fail for others after I changed my A record?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "In real-world migrations, this classic bug happens when you updated your IPv4 A record to a new web host but forgot to update or delete an old IPv6 AAAA record. Visitors on IPv6 networks get routed to your old host via the leftover AAAA record; delete or update the AAAA record to fix it immediately."
      }
    }
  ]
};

const breadcrumb = [
  ["Home", "/"],
  ["DNS Guide", "/dns/"],
  ["Record Types", "/dns-records/"],
  ["AAAA Record"]
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="AAAA Record: What an IPv6 Quad-A Record Is, Syntax, and Examples"
  description="Learn what an AAAA (Quad-A) DNS record is, why it is named AAAA, IPv6 colon-hex compression rules, Happy Eyeballs resolution, and AAAA vs A records."
  schema={[faq]}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns-records/" class="hover:text-accent">Record Types</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">AAAA Record</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">
      AAAA Record: What an IPv6 Quad-A Record Is, Syntax, and Examples
    </h1>

    <div class="pd-answer mt-6" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
        An AAAA record (pronounced <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="Quad-A: informal industry name for an AAAA record reflecting its fourfold IPv6 bit length">"Quad-A"</abbr>, or quad A record) is <strong class="text-ink">a DNS resource record that maps a domain name to a 128-bit <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="IPv6: Internet Protocol version 6, providing 128-bit addresses to replace exhausted IPv4 pools">IPv6</abbr> address (such as <code>2606:4700:4700::1111</code>) so visitors on IPv6 networks reach your server</strong>. Domains publish AAAA records beside IPv4 A records for <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="Dual-Stack: networking setup where servers and DNS simultaneously support both IPv4 and IPv6">dual-stack</abbr> routing. The quadruple-A name reflects IPv6's fourfold bit length.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Diagram name="resolution" caption="DNS resolution flow: how recursive resolvers query AAAA records for IPv6 addresses alongside IPv4 A records" />

    <nav class="pd-toc my-8" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#why-is-it-called-an-aaaa-record-and-what-does-its-syntax-look-like">Why it is called AAAA &amp; IPv6 syntax</a></li>
        <li><a href="#aaaa-record-vs-a-record-how-do-they-compare-comparison-table">AAAA vs A record comparison</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-do-dual-stack-browsers-use-aaaa-and-a-records-together-happy-eyeballs">Dual-stack &amp; Happy Eyeballs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#warning-how-a-bad-or-stale-aaaa-record-breaks-your-website-ssl-and-email">How stale AAAA records break sites</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-to-add-check-and-verify-an-aaaa-record-dig-nslookup-and-browser-doh">How to add &amp; verify an AAAA record</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="why-is-it-called-an-aaaa-record-and-what-does-its-syntax-look-like">Why Is It Called an AAAA Record and What Does Its Syntax Look Like?</h2>
      <p>
        An AAAA record (pronounced "Quad-A", IANA Type 28, representing the official DNS AAAA record full form) <strong class="text-ink">takes its four-letter name from the fact that a 128-bit IPv6 address is exactly four times the bit length of a 32-bit IPv4 <code>A</code> (Address) record</strong>. Engineers exploring why is it called aaaa record discover that standard IPv4 infrastructure provides 4.3 billion addresses written in dotted-decimal notation (<code>93.184.216.34</code>), whereas IPv6 provides 340 undecillion addresses written as eight 16-bit <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="Hexadecimal: base-16 numeral system using digits 0-9 and letters a-f to represent IPv6 groups">hexadecimal</abbr> groups separated by colons (<code>2606:2800:220:1:248:1893:25c8:1946</code>). The table below defines the 4 zone fields, wire specifications, and official IPv6 shorthand compression rules of an AAAA record.
      </p>

      <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink-dim">
        The following production AAAA record example demonstrates both expanded syntax and zero-compressed notation in zone file format:
      </p>

      <pre class="my-4 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-accent-soft leading-relaxed"><code>; Hostname           TTL    Class  Type   128-Bit IPv6 Address (Value)
example.com.         3600   IN     AAAA   2606:2800:220:1:248:1893:25c8:1946
dns.example.com.     3600   IN     AAAA   2606:4700:4700::1111</code></pre>

      <section class="not-prose my-6" aria-label="AAAA record specification table">
        <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Field / Rule</th>
                <th>Specification</th>
                <th>Example / Protocol Constraint</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">Official Record Name</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">IPv6 Address Record (AAAA)</td>
                <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Standardized in RFC 3596; replaces obsolete experimental A6 records.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">IANA Type Code</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">Type 28 (Decimal)</td>
                <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Queried by resolvers requesting an IPv6 address endpoint.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">Value / RDATA Format</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">128-bit IPv6 Address (16 bytes)</td>
                <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">8 groups of 4 hexadecimal digits (0-9, a-f) separated by colons (:).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">Rule 1: Leading Zeros</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">Omit Leading Group Zeros</td>
                <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The sequence :0220:0001:0248: compresses to :220:1:248: (RFC 5952).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">Rule 2: Double-Colon (::)</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">Collapse Contiguous Zeros</td>
                <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">One run of consecutive all-zero groups collapses to :: at most once per address.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">Coexistence &amp; Apex (@)</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">Root (@) &amp; Subdomains</td>
                <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Coexists with A, MX, TXT, NS, SOA; conflicts with a <a href="/cname-record/">CNAME Record</a>.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <p>
        Modern DNS control panels accept both full 39-character uncompressed IPv6 strings and RFC 5952 zero-compressed strings (<code>::</code>), normalizing characters to lowercase automatically. Validate, expand, or compress IPv6 addresses with our <a href="/ipv4-to-ipv6-converter/">IPv4 to IPv6 Converter</a>, and inspect active records globally using the live <a href="/aaaa-record-lookup/">AAAA Record Lookup (IPv6)</a> tool.
      </p>

      <h2 id="aaaa-record-vs-a-record-how-do-they-compare-comparison-table">AAAA Record vs A Record: How Do They Compare? (Comparison Table)</h2>
      <p>
        An AAAA record and an <a href="/a-record/">A Record</a> perform <strong class="text-ink">the exact same hostname-to-IP translation job for two different internet protocol generations: A records map domains to 32-bit IPv4 addresses, while AAAA records map domains to 128-bit IPv6 addresses</strong>. Comparing an A record vs AAAA record reveals identical routing roles operated across distinct transport protocols. The table below compares A and AAAA records across 7 technical dimensions.
      </p>

      <section class="not-prose my-6" aria-label="AAAA record vs A record comparison table">
        <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Feature / Dimension</th>
                <th>A Record (IPv4)</th>
                <th>AAAA Record (IPv6)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">IP Protocol Version</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">IPv4 (Internet Protocol v4)</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">IPv6 (Internet Protocol v6)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">Address Bit Length</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">32 bits (4 bytes)</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">128 bits (16 bytes - 4x bit length)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">Total Address Space</td>
                <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">~4.29 billion (2^32 addresses)</td>
                <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">~340 undecillion (2^128 addresses)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">Address Syntax</td>
                <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">4 decimal octets (93.184.216.34)</td>
                <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">8 colon-hex groups (2606:4700:4700::1111)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">IANA DNS Type Code</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">Type 1 (RFC 1035)</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">Type 28 (RFC 3596)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">Reverse DNS (PTR) Zone</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">.in-addr.arpa (Reversed octets)</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">.ip6.arpa (Reversed 32 hex nibbles)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="font-medium text-ink">SPF Record Mechanism</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">ip4: (e.g., ip4:192.0.2.0/24)</td>
                <td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">ip6: (e.g., ip6:2001:db8::/32)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <p>
        The lack of native backward compatibility between IPv4 and IPv6 means an IPv4-only client cannot connect directly to an AAAA address, while an IPv6-only client requires an AAAA record or carrier-level NAT64 translation. Modern web architectures publish both an A record (verifiable via our <a href="/a-record-lookup/">A Record Lookup</a>) and an AAAA record on identical hostnames. Review architectural tradeoffs in depth in our guide to <a href="/ipv4-vs-ipv6/">IPv4 vs IPv6</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-do-dual-stack-browsers-use-aaaa-and-a-records-together-happy-eyeballs">How Do Dual-Stack Browsers Use AAAA and A Records Together? (Happy Eyeballs)</h2>
      <p>
        Modern dual-stack web browsers and operating systems resolve AAAA and A records simultaneously using <strong class="text-ink">the <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="Happy Eyeballs (RFC 8305): algorithm connecting IPv6 and IPv4 in parallel to minimize connection delay">Happy Eyeballs</abbr> algorithm (RFC 8305) across 5 connection-racing steps</strong> so users receive the fastest available network path:
      </p>

      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6">
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Transmit</strong> two parallel DNS queries from the client device (<code>QTYPE=AAAA</code> for IPv6 and <code>QTYPE=A</code> for IPv4) to the recursive resolver at the exact same millisecond.
        </li>
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Prioritize</strong> the <code>AAAA</code> response first when both records arrive, granting IPv6 a 50-millisecond resolution window before falling back if the IPv6 DNS response experiences network delay.
        </li>
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Initiate</strong> a TCP (SYN) or QUIC handshake to the server's IPv6 address first, starting a 250-millisecond connection head-start timer.
        </li>
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Launch</strong> a parallel fallback connection to the server's IPv4 address if the IPv6 handshake does not complete within 250 milliseconds.
        </li>
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Use</strong> whichever connection completes the initial handshake first and terminate the slower runner-up socket immediately.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Happy Eyeballs ensures optimal routing for over 45% of global mobile and fiber internet connections that default to IPv6 when an AAAA record exists. This design avoids mobile carrier-grade NAT congestion while automatically protecting visitors from connection delays if local IPv6 routing fails.
      </p>

      <h2 id="warning-how-a-bad-or-stale-aaaa-record-breaks-your-website-ssl-and-email">Warning: How a Bad or Stale AAAA Record Breaks Your Website, SSL, and Email</h2>
      <p>
        Publishing an unreachable or leftover AAAA record causes <strong class="text-ink">4 severe production issues across website load times, SSL certificate validation, outbound email bounces, and web server routing</strong>:
      </p>

      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6">
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Stale AAAA Records Pointing to Old Web Hosts Cause SSL Errors:</strong> Migrating a website to a new hosting provider while updating only the IPv4 A record leaves the old AAAA record pointing to the previous host. Dual-stack client browsers running Happy Eyeballs prioritize IPv6, causing visitors on mobile and fiber networks to land on the retired host and encounter <code>NET::ERR_CERT_COMMON_NAME_INVALID</code> (detailed in our guide to <a href="/ssl-certificate-errors/">SSL Certificate Errors</a>), while IPv4 users see the new site.
        </li>
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Web Servers Missing IPv6 Listen Directives Drop Connections:</strong> Publishing an AAAA record when NGINX or Apache listens exclusively on IPv4 (<code>listen 443 ssl;</code>) without the corresponding IPv6 socket directive (<code>listen [::]:443 ssl;</code>) forces every IPv6 visitor to experience a 250 ms connection timeout before falling back to IPv4, or serves the wrong default virtual host.
        </li>
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Outbound IPv6 Mail Without Matching Reverse DNS Rejects Messages (550-5.7.25):</strong> Mail servers attempt outbound delivery to Gmail (<code>mx.google.com</code>) over IPv6 whenever an IPv6 interface exists. Google strictly requires every sending IPv6 address to carry a valid reverse DNS <a href="/ptr-record/">PTR Record</a> under <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="ip6.arpa: special DNS zone managing reverse DNS pointer records for IPv6 addresses"><code>.ip6.arpa</code></abbr> that resolves back to the sending hostname. Missing reverse DNS records trigger permanent rejections with error <code>550-5.7.25</code>. Audit your IPv6 reverse DNS using our live <a href="/ptr-record-lookup/">PTR (Reverse DNS) Lookup</a>.
        </li>
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Omitting ip6: in Your SPF Record Breaks Email Authentication:</strong> Transmitting outbound email over an IPv6 interface with only <code>ip4:</code> declared in your <a href="/spf-record/">SPF Record</a> causes SPF validation to fail at receiving servers. Production email setups include their IPv6 address or subnet using the <code>ip6:</code> mechanism (such as <code>v=spf1 ip4:198.51.100.10 ip6:2001:db8:1234::/48 -all</code>).
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Zone administrators adhere to this operational principle: only publish an AAAA record if your origin web server or CDN proxy actively listens on IPv6 and serves your SSL certificate on <code>[::]:443</code>. Operating an IPv4-only server with no AAAA record preserves faster, error-free connectivity compared to publishing a broken IPv6 endpoint.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-to-add-check-and-verify-an-aaaa-record-dig-nslookup-and-browser-doh">How to Add, Check, and Verify an AAAA Record (dig, nslookup, and Browser DoH)</h2>
      <p>
        Publish and verify an AAAA record for your domain by completing <strong class="text-ink">4 configuration and diagnostic steps across your DNS control panel and terminal</strong>:
      </p>

      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6">
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Confirm</strong> that your web server or load balancer possesses an assigned public IPv6 address and responds over HTTPS by executing <code>curl -6 -Iv https://[2001:db8::1]</code> prior to creating DNS records.
        </li>
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Create</strong> a new record in your DNS control panel with Type set to <code>AAAA</code>, Host set to <code>@</code> (for root) or the subdomain (<code>www</code>), Value set to the 128-bit IPv6 address, and <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="Time to Live (TTL): cache duration in seconds across DNS resolvers">TTL</abbr> set to <code>3600</code> seconds.
        </li>
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Query</strong> the published record from your command prompt using <code>dig</code> or <code>nslookup</code> to confirm the compressed IPv6 answer:
          <pre class="my-4 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-accent-soft leading-relaxed"><code># Query AAAA record on Linux / macOS:
dig example.com AAAA +short

# Query AAAA record on Windows Command Prompt:
nslookup -type=aaaa example.com 1.1.1.1

# Test end-to-end IPv6 HTTPS connection to the domain:
curl -6 -Iv https://example.com</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li class="leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Verify</strong> global resolution consistency across worldwide DNS checkpoints with our <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">DNS Propagation Checker</a>.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Resolving these verification commands confirms end-to-end IPv6 readiness from authoritative nameservers down to the application layer. Maintaining strict parity between A records, AAAA records, reverse DNS PTR mappings, and SPF authentication mechanisms ensures robust domain accessibility across the global IPv6 internet. Review complete protocol specifications across all resource records in our directory of <a href="/dns-records/">DNS Record Types</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What does AAAA stand for in DNS?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">In DNS, the AAAA record full form stands for a fourfold Address record (pronounced "Quad-A"). The IETF named the 128-bit IPv6 address record AAAA (32 bits x 4 = 128 bits) because a single A record stores a 32-bit IPv4 address.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do I need both an A record and an AAAA record for my website?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes. Publish both an A record (for IPv4) and an AAAA record (for IPv6) on the same hostname when your web server or CDN supports IPv6. This dual-stack setup lets visitors on modern mobile and fiber IPv6 networks connect directly while keeping full compatibility for IPv4-only users.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What happens if my domain only has an A record and no AAAA record?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">In practice, your website still works normally for 99.9% of internet users. Almost all consumer ISPs and mobile carriers run dual-stack networks or NAT64 gateways, so when a visitor's browser sees no AAAA record, it immediately connects to your IPv4 A record with zero error.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why does my website load for some people and fail for others after I changed my A record?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">In real-world migrations, this classic bug happens when you updated your IPv4 A record to a new web host but forgot to update or delete an old IPv6 AAAA record. Visitors on IPv6 networks get routed to your old host via the leftover AAAA record; delete or update the AAAA record to fix it immediately.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
